1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,906
Everything up to this point has been one piece at a time: a brief here, a batch

2
00:00:04,906 --> 00:00:07,386
there, a testing architecture in Section 7.

3
00:00:07,386 --> 00:00:12,114
The capstone asks you to run all of it, in sequence, on one real product, the same

4
00:00:12,114 --> 00:00:14,940
sequence Section 10.4's ninety-day plan laid out.

5
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:19,669
Pick a product you can actually get creative rights to and real, even if small, ad

6
00:00:19,669 --> 00:00:20,476
spend against.

7
00:00:20,476 --> 00:00:22,437
Your own business if you have one.

8
00:00:22,437 --> 00:00:24,225
An employer's, with permission.

9
00:00:24,225 --> 00:00:27,800
A friend's small brand, if they'll let you run a genuine test.

10
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:32,068
This only teaches you something if the product and the test are both real.

11
00:00:32,068 --> 00:00:36,739
A hypothetical product produces a hypothetical result, teaching nothing about the

12
00:00:36,739 --> 00:00:38,412
honest reads Section 8 built.

13
00:00:38,412 --> 00:00:43,256
Work through the sequence exactly as taught: product truth doc, hook taxonomy, angle

14
00:00:43,256 --> 00:00:47,754
map, batch generation, pre-flight checklist, testing architecture, and a first

15
00:00:47,754 --> 00:00:48,446
honest read.

16
00:00:48,446 --> 00:00:53,232
Sections 11.2 and 11.3 walk the same sequence on Harlan's account immediately after

17
00:00:53,232 --> 00:00:57,846
this, as a worked example to check your own decisions against, not a template to

18
00:00:57,846 --> 00:00:59,115
copy without thinking.

19
00:00:59,115 --> 00:01:03,440
This is deliberately the hardest lecture in the course to simply listen to.

20
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:06,496
Everything before it could be understood by watching.

21
00:01:06,496 --> 00:01:07,361
This one can't.

22
00:01:07,361 --> 00:01:11,975
The capstone only teaches you anything once you've actually opened Section 2.1's

23
00:01:11,975 --> 00:01:16,300
product truth doc template and started filling it in with your own numbers.

24
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:20,842
Notice this table is identical in shape to Section 10.4's checklist.

25
00:01:20,842 --> 00:01:21,899
That's deliberate.

26
00:01:21,899 --> 00:01:26,772
The capstone isn't new content, it's the proof that the sequence you've been taught

27
00:01:26,772 --> 00:01:31,645
actually holds together end to end, on a product this course has never seen before.

28
00:01:31,645 --> 00:01:36,518
Expect this to take real time, genuinely days rather than the length of this video.

29
00:01:36,518 --> 00:01:41,332
Generating and pre-flighting a proper batch alone is several hours of honest work.

30
00:01:41,332 --> 00:01:45,970
Running a test long enough to get past Section 7.4's small-sample honesty takes

31
00:01:45,970 --> 00:01:48,553
actual days of live spend, not an afternoon.

32
00:01:48,553 --> 00:01:53,250
Budget for that before you start, not partway through when the deadline pressure

33
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:53,778
shows up.

34
00:01:53,778 --> 00:01:58,592
Notice, too, that this table has no line for "pitching" or "reporting." Those come

35
00:01:58,592 --> 00:02:03,171
later, in Section 10.4's phase three, once a real cycle has actually been run.

36
00:02:03,171 --> 00:02:07,868
The capstone stops at an honest first read, which is exactly as far as a genuine

37
00:02:07,868 --> 00:02:12,624
first sprint should go before anything gets scaled, refreshed, or shown to anyone

38
00:02:12,624 --> 00:02:14,033
outside the work itself.

39
00:02:14,583 --> 00:02:17,798
Five checkpoints, and notice none of them require a winner.

40
00:02:17,798 --> 00:02:22,157
A capstone that ends honestly in noise, correctly identified as noise, has still

41
00:02:22,157 --> 00:02:22,702
succeeded.

42
00:02:22,702 --> 00:02:25,862
Section 8.2 already told you most real batches land there.

43
00:02:25,862 --> 00:02:30,222
Demanding a winner from your own capstone would be asking the exercise to lie to

44
00:02:30,222 --> 00:02:30,440
you.

45
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:34,581
What actually fails the capstone is skipping a checkpoint, not landing on an

46
00:02:34,581 --> 00:02:35,616
unglamorous result.

47
00:02:35,616 --> 00:02:39,976
A batch that skipped pre-flight, or a test read before enough spend accumulated,

48
00:02:39,976 --> 00:02:44,226
teaches you nothing true about your own product, whatever verdict it produces.

49
00:02:44,226 --> 00:02:48,803
Would you trust a verdict from a process that skipped one of these five checkpoints?

50
00:02:48,803 --> 00:02:53,272
If the honest answer is no, hold your own capstone to the same standard you'd hold

51
00:02:53,272 --> 00:02:54,035
anyone else's.

52
00:02:54,035 --> 00:02:58,448
The rubric in Section 11.4 checks exactly this, but the more useful check happens

53
00:02:58,448 --> 00:03:00,465
here, honestly, before you get there.

54
00:03:01,015 --> 00:03:05,336
The next two lectures walk this exact sequence on Harlan's account, in detail, as

55
00:03:05,336 --> 00:03:06,350
the worked example.

56
00:03:06,350 --> 00:03:10,725
Watch them before you start if you want a model to follow, or after your own first

57
00:03:10,725 --> 00:03:15,046
pass if you'd rather work it out independently first and compare notes afterward.

58
00:03:15,046 --> 00:03:16,060
Either order works.

59
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:20,328
What matters is that your own capstone gets run for real, on a real product, not

60
00:03:20,328 --> 00:03:22,409
just watched as someone else's example.

61
00:03:22,409 --> 00:03:25,876
That's the entire difference this course has been built to teach.

62
00:03:25,876 --> 00:03:30,305
Give yourself permission for this to take longer than the two worked lectures do to

63
00:03:30,305 --> 00:03:30,625
watch.

64
00:03:30,625 --> 00:03:34,786
Harlan's story compresses months of real account history into a few minutes of

65
00:03:34,786 --> 00:03:36,493
narration for teaching purposes.

66
00:03:36,493 --> 00:03:40,975
Your own capstone doesn't get that compression, and expecting it to move at the same

67
00:03:40,975 --> 00:03:45,296
pace as the video is the fastest way to feel behind on a schedule nobody actually

68
00:03:45,296 --> 00:03:45,937
set for you.
